---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MarketplaceBreedersList from '../../components/MarketplaceBreedersList.astro';
import { fetchBreeders } from '../../lib/marketplaceData';

const breeders = await fetchBreeders('SHEEP');

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Find Breeders', url: '/find-breeders' },
  { name: 'Sheep Breeders', url: '/find-breeders/sheep' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What sheep breeds and operations are on BreederHQ?',
    answer: 'Wool and fiber breeds (Shetland, Romney, Merino, BFL, Wensleydale, Cormo, CVM, Rambouillet), meat breeds (Suffolk, Hampshire, Dorper, Katahdin, Texel, Southdown), dairy breeds (East Friesian, Lacaune, Awassi), heritage and rare breeds, and dual-purpose flocks are all welcome. NSIP participation and breed-specific registries are supported.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I see NSIP performance data on sheep profiles?',
    answer: 'Yes, where breeders are enrolled. The National Sheep Improvement Program (NSIP) publishes EBVs (Estimated Breeding Values) for growth traits, maternal traits, wool traits, and parasite resistance. NSIP-enrolled rams are among the most valuable genetics in the sheep industry because EBVs predict how their offspring will perform, not just what one animal looks like at one point in time. Ask any seedstock breeder whether they use NSIP.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What health certifications matter for sheep buyers?',
    answer: 'Scrapie Free Flock Certification (USDA program) is important for any breeding stock purchase. OPP (Ovine Progressive Pneumonia) is a viral disease with no cure that spreads through colostrum and milk; responsible breeders test and manage for it. Foot rot and foot scald history matter for flocks moving to wet climates. Ask breeders for whole-flock test results and biosecurity protocol, not just the records on the individual animal being sold.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I know about buying fiber sheep?',
    answer: 'For fiber breeds, micron count is the most important quality metric. A lower micron means finer, softer fleece, but selecting purely for fine fleece often comes at a cost to fleece weight and staple length. Independent fiber analysis from labs like Yocom-McColl gives objective numbers. NSIP EBVs for fiber traits are especially valuable for Rambouillet, Merino, and CVM programs. For hand spinners, ask about natural color genetics, staple length, and whether the breeder maintains individual fleece records by animal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I find lambs, breeding stock, and raw fleece all on BreederHQ?',
    answer: 'You can find registered ewes, rams, and lambs from breeders with documented records. Some breeders also list raw fleece through their profile. Use the breed and intent filters on the animals page to narrow results by what you are looking for.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Find Responsible Sheep Breeders | BreederHQ"
  description="Find responsible sheep breeders for wool, meat, dairy, and heritage breeds: NSIP EBVs, scrapie certification, OPP testing, and fiber analysis. Browse BreederHQ."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-white">
    <a href="#main" class="skip-link sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-4 focus:left-4 focus:z-50 focus:px-4 focus:py-2 focus:bg-white focus:text-black focus:shadow-lg focus:rounded">Skip to content</a>

    <section id="main" class="py-16 px-4 bg-gradient-to-b from-stone-50 to-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Find responsible sheep breeders</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          NSIP EBVs, Scrapie Free Flock Certification, OPP testing, and fiber analysis data
          for wool, meat, dairy, and heritage programs, published from real herd records.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="https://marketplace.breederhq.com/breeders" class="bg-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-lg text-lg font-semibold hover:bg-[hsl(24,95%,48%)] transition w-full sm:w-auto">Browse Sheep Breeders</a>
          <a href="https://marketplace.breederhq.com/animals" class="bg-white border-2 border-stone-300 text-stone-800 px-8 py-4 rounded-lg text-lg font-semibold hover:border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] transition w-full sm:w-auto">Browse Available Sheep</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <MarketplaceBreedersList breeders={breeders} speciesLabel="sheep" />

    <section class="py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-10 text-center">
          What the indicators on a breeder profile mean
        </h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Identity confirmation</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">"Identity confirmed via Stripe Identity" means Stripe (not BreederHQ) confirmed the breeder's government-issued ID matched the legal name on file. BreederHQ does not perform the verification itself.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Registry passthrough</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">"Confirmed via NSIP" (or breed-specific registries) means BreederHQ queried the registry and found a matching record. Click the source-record link to verify current status directly with NSIP or the breed association.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Breeder's own attestation</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Scrapie certification status, OPP testing, fiber analysis data, and health records are the breeder's own attestation. BreederHQ does not open, read, or vouch for uploaded documents. Request originals and verify directly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-center mt-6">
          <a href="/trust" class="text-emerald-700 hover:underline text-sm font-medium">Read the full Identity &amp; Credentials policy &rarr;</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 px-4 bg-stone-50">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">What you can see on a sheep breeder profile</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-lg text-gray-700">
          <li><strong>NSIP EBVs:</strong> Estimated Breeding Values for growth, maternal, fleece, and parasite-resistance traits for enrolled breeders.</li>
          <li><strong>Scrapie and disease documentation:</strong> Scrapie Free Flock Certification status, OPP testing results, foot rot history, and biosecurity protocols.</li>
          <li><strong>Registered pedigrees:</strong> breed-specific association registration with documented lineage and show records on ancestors.</li>
          <li><strong>Lambing and production history:</strong> past lambing records, current pregnancies, expected lambs, and waitlist status.</li>
          <li><strong>Fiber quality data:</strong> for fiber breeds, independent lab micron counts, staple length, fleece weight, color, and natural color genetics where the breeder records them.</li>
          <li><strong>Program story and goals:</strong> what the breeder is selecting for, herd history, and photos of current animals.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">How to evaluate the sheep breeders you find</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <a href="/buyers/evaluate-breeders" class="block bg-white border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 hover:border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:shadow-sm transition">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How to Evaluate Breeders</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">The questions to ask any livestock breeder, and what separates responsible programs from the rest.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/buyers/health-tests" class="block bg-white border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 hover:border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:shadow-sm transition">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Understanding Health Testing</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Why scrapie certification, OPP testing, and whole-flock documentation matter before you buy.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/buyers/red-flags" class="block bg-white border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 hover:border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:shadow-sm transition">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Red Flags to Avoid</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Warning signs of irresponsible practices and how to protect your flock before introducing new animals.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/buyers/contracts-and-agreements" class="block bg-white border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 hover:border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:shadow-sm transition">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Contracts and Agreements</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">What a responsible livestock purchase agreement should cover before you commit.</p>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 px-4 bg-gradient-to-br from-stone-900 to-stone-800">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Ready to find your sheep?</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-stone-300 mb-8">Browse responsible sheep breeders on the BreederHQ Marketplace.</p>
        <a href="https://marketplace.breederhq.com/breeders" class="inline-block bg-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-lg text-lg font-semibold hover:bg-[hsl(24,95%,48%)] transition">Browse Sheep Breeders</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
